  <body>
    <title><p>The Lighthouse at Sker Point</p></title>
    <section>
      <title><p>1. The Keeper's Ledger</p></title>
      <p>Maren wrote the log in a hand so small it looked like a line of ants.</p>
      <p>The wind had been steady for nine days, and the entries repeated</p>
      <p>themselves: clear at four, clear at eight, nothing on the horizon.</p>
      <p>She had inherited the ledger with the post. Nobody had explained the</p>
      <p>job, only the objects - a brass key, a tin of wick, a book with a</p>
      <p>leather spine and no title. The keeper's rule was simple and unwritten:</p>
      <p>the light does not go out.</p>
      <p>On the eleventh day the wind changed direction, and with it came a sound</p>
      <p>she had never heard before, low and patient, like something large moving</p>
      <p>slowly under the water of the sound.</p>
      <subtitle>Section 1 of 5</subtitle>
    </section>
    <section>
      <title><p>2. Fog on the Sound</p></title>
      <p>Fog arrived without ceremony. It simply was there at noon, a soft grey</p>
      <p>wall that swallowed the harbour and then the last hundred metres of rock</p>
      <p>below the tower.</p>
      <p>In fog the work became ritual. Wick trimmed, lens polished, clock wound,</p>
      <p>log signed. Maren found she liked the ritual. It gave the hours an order</p>
      <p>the fog had taken away.</p>
      <p>She talked to the sea a little. It was not madness, exactly; it was the</p>
      <p>ordinary courtesy of living alone with something that listens. The sea</p>
      <p>never answered, but it never left either.</p>
      <empty-line/>
      <poem><stanza><v>Wick trimmed, lens polished,</v><v>clock wound, log signed, lamp turned.</v></stanza></poem>
      <subtitle>Section 2 of 5</subtitle>
    </section>
    <section>
      <title><p>3. What the Lamp Recorded</p></title>
      <p>The lens was a machine of brass and glass that turned on a clockwork</p>
      <p>drum. Maren cleaned it the way she imagined her grandmother had, with a</p>
      <p>soft cloth and a great deal of patience.</p>
      <p>Behind the glass the flame flattened and widened. She wrote down the</p>
      <p>time, the weather, the number of ships that answered. The numbers</p>
      <p>mattered. Somebody, somewhere, was reading them.</p>
      <p>That night a ship answered. Three short flashes, meaning all well. Maren</p>
      <p>wrote it down, and then sat for a while in the dark, letting the beam go</p>
      <p>over her face again and again.</p>
      <subtitle>Section 3 of 5</subtitle>
    </section>
    <section>
      <title><p>4. The Long Dark Interval</p></title>
      <p>The dark interval began on a Thursday and lasted four days. The</p>
      <p>generator failed, the spare coil was corroded, and the road was under</p>
      <p>water.</p>
      <p>She trimmed wick by hand every four hours through the long nights,</p>
      <p>moving the lamp with a rope and a shoulder, holding the light on the</p>
      <p>channel the way she had been taught - a foot left of the marker, no</p>
      <p>more, no less.</p>
      <p>The ships kept coming in. She counted nine of them, and she never once</p>
      <p>wrote the word failure in the ledger, because it had not happened. The</p>
      <p>light was still there. That was the whole of the job.</p>
      <subtitle>Section 4 of 5</subtitle>
    </section>
    <section>
      <title><p>5. A Light Kept Anyway</p></title>
      <p>The relief boat came in spring. Maren handed over the key, the tin, and</p>
      <p>the book with the leather spine, and said nothing about the eleven days</p>
      <p>of wind or the ship that answered.</p>
      <p>The new keeper read the ledger for a long time. At the entry for the</p>
      <p>thirty-first night he stopped and looked up, and Maren told him what she</p>
      <p>had done with the rope, and the foot left of the marker.</p>
      <p>Then she walked up the path to the bus, carrying nothing much, in a coat</p>
      <p>that smelled of oil and salt. Behind her the lamp turned, and kept</p>
      <p>turning, and the light did not go out.</p>
      <subtitle>Section 5 of 5</subtitle>
    </section>
